      <td>IBETO</td>\n                <td>₦1,580.00</td>\n            </tr>\n            \n            <tr>\n                <td>RAINOIL DELTA</td>\n                <td>₦1,600.00</td>\n            </tr>\n            \n            <tr>\n                <td>MATRIX LAGOS</td>\n                <td>₦1,580.00</td>\n            </tr>\n            \n            <tr>\n                <td>MATRIX WARRI</td>\n                <td>₦1,590.00</td>\n            </tr>\n            \n            <tr>\n                <td>DUPORT</td>\n                <td>₦1,585.00</td>\n            </tr>\n            \n            <tr>\n                <td>AFRICAN TERMINAL</td>\n                <td>₦1,585.00</td>\n            </tr>\n            \n            <tr>\n                <td>GULF TREASURE</td>\n                <td>₦1,585.00</td>\n            </tr>\n            \n            <tr>\n                <td>IBACHEM</td>\n                <td>₦1,585.00</td>\n            </tr>\n            \n\n        </table>\n\n        <br>\n        \n\n            \n        <h2 style=\"color:#d42e12;\">\n            Market News\n        </h2>\n        \n            <div\n                style=\"\n                    border:1px solid #fad5a1;\n                    padding:15px;\n                    margin-bottom:15px;\n                    border-radius:8px;\n                    \n                \"\n            >\n\n                <h3>\n                    Indigenous Firms Now Produce Over 60% of Nigeria's Crude Oil — Highest Since 1956\n                </h3>\n\n                <p>\n                     indigenous oil producers have achieved a historic milestone, accounting for more than 60 per cent of the country's crude oil production. The development marks a defining shift in the nation's petroleum industry, with local operators now taking the lead in an upstream sector once dominated by international oil companies.\n                </p>\n\n                <a\n                    href=\"https://petroleumprice.ng/news/articles/indigenous-firms-now-produce-over-60-of-nigerias-crude-oil-highest-since-1956\"\n                    target=\"_blank\"\n                    style=\"\n                        color:#d42e12;\n                        font-weight:bold;\n                        text-decoration:none;\n                    \"\n                >\n                    Full Article →\n                </a>\n\n            </div>\n            \n            <div\n                style=\"\n                    border:1px solid #fad5a1;\n                    padding:15px;\n                    margin-bottom:15px;\n                    border-radius:8px;\n                    \n                \"\n            >\n\n                <h3>\n                    Dangote’s 700,000bpd Kenya Refinery Set for Ground Breaking Before Year-End\n                </h3>\n\n                <p>\n                    Nigerian industrialist Aliko Dangote's proposed Sh2.2 trillion ($17.2 billion) oil refinery in Kenya's Lamu County expected to commence before the end of 2026. Kenyan President William Ruto announced that preparations for the 700,000-barrel-per-day refinery have reached an advanced stage. To fast-track the investment, Ruto appointed Deputy President Kithure Kindiki to chair a government committee.\n                </p>\n\n                <a\n                    href=\"https://petroleumprice.ng/news/articles/dangotes-700000bpd-kenya-refinery-set-for-ground-breaking-before-year-end\"\n                    target=\"_blank\"\n                    style=\"\n                        color:#d42e12;\n                        font-weight:bold;\n                        text-decoration:none;\n                    \"\n                >\n                    Full Article →\n                </a>\n\n            </div>\n            \n            <div\n                style=\"\n                    border:1px solid #fad5a1;\n                    padding:15px;\n                    margin-bottom:15px;\n                    border-radius:8px;\n                    \n                \"\n            >\n\n                <h3>\n                    Oil Prices Drop as Iran's Supreme Leader Decides Fate of Strait of Hormuz Reopening\n                </h3>\n\n                <p>\n                    Global oil prices traded lower on Wednesday as markets awaited a crucial decision from Iran's Supreme Leader, Ayatollah Ali Khamenei. The prospect of restoring tanker movements through the strategic waterway eased immediate supply concerns. As at the time of writing 9:16 p.m. (WAT), Brent crude traded at $79.53 per barrel, up 0.21%, while West Texas Intermediate (WTI) stood at $75.28 per barrel.\n                </p>\n\n                <a\n                    href=\"https://petroleumprice.ng/news/articles/oil-prices-drop-as-irans-supreme-leader-decides-fate-of-strait-of-hormuz-reopening\"\n                    target=\"_blank\"\n                    style=\"\n                        color:#d42e12;\n                        font-weight:bold;\n                        text-decoration:none;\n                    \"\n                >\n                    Full Article →\n                </a>\n\n            </div>\n            \n            <div\n                style=\"\n                    border:1px solid #fad5a1;\n                    padding:15px;\n                    margin-bottom:15px;\n                    border-radius:8px;\n                    \n                \"\n            >\n\n                <h3>\n                    Dangote Refinery Cuts Petrol Price to ₦1,165/L, Diesel to ₦1,570/L\n                </h3>\n\n                <p>\n                    Dangote Petroleum Refinery has reduced its ex-depot prices for petrol and diesel. The new prices, which take effect from August 6, 2026, represent a ₦50 per litre reduction. The latest adjustment places the refinery's petrol price below prevailing private depot rates in Lagos.\n                </p>\n\n                <a\n                    href=\"https://petroleumprice.ng/news/articles/dangote-refinery-cuts-petrol-price-to-1165l-diesel-to-1570l\"\n                    target=\"_blank\"\n                    style=\"\n                        color:#d42e12;\n                        font-weight:bold;\n                        text-decoration:none;\n                    \"\n                >\n                    Full Article →\n                </a>\n\n            </div>\n            \n            <div\n                style=\"\n                    border:1px solid #fad5a1;\n                    padding:15px;\n                    margin-bottom:15px;\n                    border-radius:8px;\n                    \n                \"\n            >\n\n                <h3>\n                    Oil Prices Recover as Oil Marketers Watch Fuel Price Direction\n                </h3>\n\n                <p>\n                    Oil prices rebounded on Wednesday after recent declines, prompting fresh attention across Nigeria's downstream petroleum market. As of 12:40 p.m. WAT on Wednesday , Brent crude traded at $80.40 per barrel , up 1.31%. U.S. West Texas Intermediate (WTI) rose 0.45% to $76.11 per barrel.\n                </p>\n\n                <a\n                    href=\"https://petroleumprice.ng/news/articles/oil-prices-recover-as-oil-marketers-watch-fuel-price-direction\"\n                    target=\"_blank\"\n                    style=\"\n                        color:#d42e12;\n                        font-weight:bold;\n                        text-decoration:none;\n                    \"\n                >\n                    Full Article →\n                </a>\n\n            </div>\n            \n\n        </body>\n\n        </html>\n        "
